// MDP where *every* reward is negative, for instantaneous-reward properties (R=?[I=k]).
//
// Companion to neg-reward-instantaneous-mdp.prism, which has one negative and one
// positive reward. That model leaves two bugs invisible, because the optimal value at
// the initial state happens to be the positive one, and because the states with a
// single choice are never themselves optimised over. Both need all-negative rewards
// plus k>=2 (so that the values of s=1/s=2 are read back in a later iteration) to show:
//
//  * MTBDD engine, Rmax: choices that do not exist are 0 in the MTBDD, so for s=1/s=2
//    (one choice each, against two nondeterministic choices elsewhere) the max was
//    taken over {real value, 0} and returned 0. Masking was applied for min only,
//    which sufficed while rewards were required to be non-negative.
//  * Hybrid engine, both Rmin and Rmax: rows not yet visited were flagged with a
//    negative value in the solution vector itself, so genuinely negative rewards were
//    read back as "not visited" and reset to 0.
//
// s=0 chooses between two absorbing states with rewards -5 and -1, so
// Rmin=?[I=k] = -5 and Rmax=?[I=k] = -1 for every k >= 1.

mdp

module M
	s : [0..2] init 0;
	[] s=0 -> (s'=1);
	[] s=0 -> (s'=2);
	[] s=1 -> true;
	[] s=2 -> true;
endmodule

rewards
	s=1 : -5;
	s=2 : -1;
endrewards
